<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mstislav II of Kiev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1170</span> · Also known as Mstislav Iziaslavich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mstislav II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> (Mstislav Iziaslavich) was prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, which he ruled as a separate principality from 1154, and grand prince of Kiev from 1167 to 1169. A son of Iziaslav II, he founded the line of Volhynian princes from which came <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-the-great">Roman the Great</a>. He was driven from Kiev in 1169 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a>'s coalition sacked the city, and died in 1170.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mstislav-ii-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Mstislav II of Kiev" width="320" height="623" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian manuscript illuminators, 1560-1570s Facial Chronicle (Illustrated Chronicle of Ivan the Terrible) (... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9C%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2_%D0%98%D0%B7%D1%8F%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87_%D0%B2%D1%85%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%82_%D0%B2_%D0%9A%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%B2_(1159).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
